  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"141\" data-end=\"335\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\">The nasal smear was examined microscopically to assess the presence of inflammatory cells, infectious agents, and mucus components. The following interpretations apply based on typical findings:</span></p>\n<ul data-start=\"337\" data-end=\"1329\">\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"337\" data-end=\"531\">\n<p data-start=\"339\" data-end=\"531\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"339\" data-end=\"385\">Eosinophils present in significant numbers</strong> suggest <strong data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"415\">allergic rhinitis</strong> or <strong data-start=\"419\" data-end=\"441\">nasal eosinophilia</strong>. This is commonly associated with conditions like seasonal allergies or atopic disorders.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"535\" data-end=\"685\">\n<p data-start=\"537\" data-end=\"685\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"537\" data-end=\"562\">Increased neutrophils</strong> may indicate a <strong data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"601\">bacterial infection</strong> or non-specific inflammation of the nasal mucosa (e.g., acute rhinitis, sinusitis).</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"687\" data-end=\"780\">\n<p data-start=\"689\" data-end=\"780\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"689\" data-end=\"717\">Lymphocytic predominance</strong> is often seen in <strong data-start=\"735\" data-end=\"779\">viral upper respiratory tract infections</strong>.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"782\" data-end=\"966\">\n<p data-start=\"784\" data-end=\"966\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"784\" data-end=\"808\">Presence of bacteria</strong> (without significant inflammatory response) may reflect colonization; however, if associated with neutrophils, it supports an active <strong data-start=\"942\" data-end=\"965\">bacterial infection</strong>.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"968\" data-end=\"1109\">\n<p data-start=\"970\" data-end=\"1109\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"970\" data-end=\"989\">Fungal elements</strong>, if detected, should be further investigated with special stains or cultures, especially in immunocompromised patients.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"1111\" data-end=\"1220\">\n<p data-start=\"1113\" data-end=\"1220\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"1113\" data-end=\"1131\">Abundant mucus</strong> is a non-specific finding but is commonly seen in both allergic and infectious rhinitis.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li style=\"font-size: 10px;\" data-start=\"1222\" data-end=\"1329\">\n<p data-start=\"1224\" data-end=\"1329\"><span style=\"font-size: 10px;\"><strong data-start=\"1224\" data-end=\"1250\">Red blood cells (RBCs)</strong> may be seen in cases of trauma, irritation, or ulceration of the nasal mucosa.</span></p>\n</li>\n</ul>",
